\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Naive validation versus the audit, against pressure outcomes (math; all ten in-domain-audited configurations).} Gap = excess hack gap at best-of-64: how much the judge's own scores inflate relative to true quality, in baseline standard deviations over a permutation null. Regret = accuracy left on the table versus best-possible selection. Bootstrap 95\% CIs in brackets (1.5B-blind CIs omitted: degenerate resamples from concentrated scores). Naive raw agreement ranks the ladder but cannot diagnose: the sabotaged frontier judge ($+0.35$) is indistinguishable from the honest blind 14B ($+0.37$), yet one inflates ($+0.85\sigma $, regret $0.133$) and the other damages (gap $+0.63$, regret $0.324$). The style partial separates them and identifies the failure mode.}}{4}{table.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:h2h}{{1}{4}{\textbf {Naive validation versus the audit, against pressure outcomes (math; all ten in-domain-audited configurations).} Gap = excess hack gap at best-of-64: how much the judge's own scores inflate relative to true quality, in baseline standard deviations over a permutation null. Regret = accuracy left on the table versus best-possible selection. Bootstrap 95\% CIs in brackets (1.5B-blind CIs omitted: degenerate resamples from concentrated scores). Naive raw agreement ranks the ladder but cannot diagnose: the sabotaged frontier judge ($+0.35$) is indistinguishable from the honest blind 14B ($+0.37$), yet one inflates ($+0.85\sigma $, regret $0.133$) and the other damages (gap $+0.63$, regret $0.324$). The style partial separates them and identifies the failure mode}{table.caption.7}{}}
